  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A marginal risk of flash flooding remains across\nNorth Carolina and Virginia for this evening.\n\nWhile a frontal boundary slowly moves east of the Blue Ridge\nthis afternoon, an upper level trough overhead will ensure more\nlift and instability available for showers and thunderstorms to\ndevelop. The overall flash flood risk has decreased compared to\nprevious days, but there still remains a marginal risk across\nNorth Carolina and Virginia this evening. The 12Z RNK sounding\nthis morning revealed a precipitable water value of 1.36 inches,\nwhich is notably lower compared to the juicy 1.95 inches from \n24 hours ago but still elevated for this time of year. Because \nof this reduction in moisture and after coordination with \nneighboring offices, no areal flood watch will be hoisted for \nthis afternoon. However, a flash flood watch remains in effect \nthrough this evening in coordination with public officials for \nsome of the dams within the headwaters of the Ararat River given\nthe heavy rainfall that took place there over the past weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather will persist through the rest\nof this week with daily chances of showers and thunderstorms.\n\nAn upper level low will close off from the main upper level \ntrough over the southern Appalachian Mountains by Tuesday\nmorning. This low should eventually weaken towards the middle of\nthis week as a large upper level ridge builds over Bermuda and\nanother upper level trough approaches the Great Lakes. While\nTuesday appears to be a lull in convective activity, moisture\nwill converge again along the Blue Ridge to bring a daily threat\nof showers and thunderstorms for the rest of the week. The\nair mass still remains moist with dewpoints ranging from the \nmid 60s to the lower 70s, so rainfall efficiency will continue \nto remain rather high. Heavy downpours from stronger storms \nrepeatedly tracking over the same locations could trigger more \nhydrological issues towards the latter half of this week as a \ncold front arrives from the west.",
